\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {An actionable protocol for the future agenda}: four measurable quantities, their definitions, and concrete testbeds. The first anchors the advantage-of-prediction question ($\delta $) on LIBERO / CALVIN-style suites; the second targets the near-unmeasured counterfactual capability; the third quantifies the prediction-to-action gap the bridges expose; the fourth audits per-capability contrast coverage across the landscape. Each is reported as a distribution or curve, never a single headline number.\relax }}{5}{table.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:future_metrics}{{6}{5}{\textbf {An actionable protocol for the future agenda}: four measurable quantities, their definitions, and concrete testbeds. The first anchors the advantage-of-prediction question ($\delta $) on LIBERO / CALVIN-style suites; the second targets the near-unmeasured counterfactual capability; the third quantifies the prediction-to-action gap the bridges expose; the fourth audits per-capability contrast coverage across the landscape. Each is reported as a distribution or curve, never a single headline number.\relax }{table.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The term ``world model'' spans two orthogonal axes the literature routinely conflates: a \emph  {representation} axis (what the model is: latent-state dynamics, action-conditioned video, or generative video, a soft boundary since action-conditioned generators straddle WAM and WFM) and a \emph  {role} axis (what it is used for: a neural simulator, or the object a benchmark scores). Columns record whether a sense is \textbf  {act}ion-\textbf  {cond}itioned, is used in a closed \textbf  {loop}, carries a standard evaluation \textbf  {metric}, and is readily \textbf  {VLA-comp}arable under one protocol (\textcolor {ForestGreen}{{\fontfamily  {pzd}\fontencoding  {U}\fontseries  {m}\fontshape  {n}\selectfont  \char 52}}\ yes, \textcolor {orange!90!black}{\textbf  {\textasciitilde }}\ partial, \textcolor {BrickRed}{{\fontfamily  {pzd}\fontencoding  {U}\fontseries  {m}\fontshape  {n}\selectfont  \char 56}}\ no, \textcolor {gray!65}{\textbf  {-{}-}}\ n/a). No single sense is action-conditioned, closed-loop, and VLA-comparable at once, which is why a benchmark that contrasts a world-model policy with a VLA policy per capability (\S  \ref {sec:gap}) is still missing. Evaluation benchmarks score the world model as an \emph  {object}; they are not world models acting as judges.\relax }}{5}{table.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:wm_senses}{{8}{5}{The term ``world model'' spans two orthogonal axes the literature routinely conflates: a \emph {representation} axis (what the model is: latent-state dynamics, action-conditioned video, or generative video, a soft boundary since action-conditioned generators straddle WAM and WFM) and a \emph {role} axis (what it is used for: a neural simulator, or the object a benchmark scores). Columns record whether a sense is \textbf {act}ion-\textbf {cond}itioned, is used in a closed \textbf {loop}, carries a standard evaluation \textbf {metric}, and is readily \textbf {VLA-comp}arable under one protocol (\yy \ yes, \pp \ partial, \nn \ no, \dm \ n/a). No single sense is action-conditioned, closed-loop, and VLA-comparable at once, which is why a benchmark that contrasts a world-model policy with a VLA policy per capability (\S \ref {sec:gap}) is still missing. Evaluation benchmarks score the world model as an \emph {object}; they are not world models acting as judges.\relax }{table.caption.6}{}}
